# integrity.json pins SHA-256 over on-disk bytes, so every checkout must be
# byte-identical on every platform — LF everywhere, no exceptions. The Windows
# .ps1 executors self-verify their own byte content (hash-of-self, exact-length
# here-strings), so CRLF would break their native self-test; they run under
# PowerShell 7, which reads LF fine. This is the deliberate reason roundhouse
# does NOT follow the usual "CRLF for Windows script types" convention.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch their bytes.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# integrity.json is GENERATED, so merging it textually is meaningless: two
# branches that each touched a hashed file produce different hashes for the
# same files, and git reports a conflict with no correct textual resolution.
# Stacked PRs made this constant - every branch touches it, so every merge to
# main conflicts every other branch by construction.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps our side rather than conflicting. That is NOT the
# correctness guarantee: `scripts/preflight` regenerates the manifest and fails
# if it differs from what is staged, so a stale or wrong manifest cannot reach
# a push regardless of what the merge picked. The attribute removes the noise;
# the gate keeps the truth.
#
# Requires the stock one-liner below, which `scripts/preflight` sets for you:
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
# Without it git falls back to a normal conflict - noisier, never wrong.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to the plugin manifests: a version conflict is a
# semantic decision about which release this is, and silently keeping one side
# would hide an intended bump.
plugins/roundhouse/integrity.json merge=ours
